    "replay-tooltip": "Replay · uses credits",
    "expanded": true,
    "full-text": "Why the best interfaces disappear: a short history of calm technology, from Mark Weiser’s ubiquitous computing to today’s ambient assistants. In 1991 Weiser wrote that the most profound technologies are those that disappear; they weave themselves into the fabric of everyday life until they are indistinguishable from it. Thirty years later, the screens around us are louder than ever. This essay looks at what it would take to build software that respects attention: interfaces that wait, that speak only when spoken to, and that leave the room as gracefully as they enter it. We start with the thermostat, move through the e-reader and the smart speaker, and end with a modest proposal for notifications that expire on their own.",
